Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Reber Grammar", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"d073459d-127b-439c-b315-94de6963b699"],

Cell["DFA with associative transition memory", "Subtitle",ExpressionUUID->"f750cfbf-f671-4a6e-a469-bf2629804557"],

Cell["\<\
Reber\[CloseCurlyQuote]s grammar (1967) is a small finite-state grammar over \
the letters T, P, S, X and V. Strings start with B and end with E. In \
artificial grammar learning experiments, participants who memorized strings \
generated by the grammar could later tell grammatical from ungrammatical \
strings, without being able to state the rules. The grammar has since become \
a standard benchmark for sequence learning in neural networks. Every letter \
labels two different transitions, so the next letter depends on the current \
state, not just on the letter before it.

This notebook implements the grammar as a finite automaton in a \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]transition\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] memory component. \
The query is a bundle of the current state and the current letter, and the \
memory returns the next state. The state is fed back and bundled with the \
next letter. The grammar is cyclic: every string starts and ends in the state \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]end\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], which is presented \
together with the first letter of each string.

Each of the twelve rules is taught once. The automaton then classifies \
grammatical strings generated by the grammar and ungrammatical strings \
derived from other grammatical strings by substituting a single letter. A \
string is accepted if the automaton reaches the state \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]end\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] after its last letter. An \
undefined transition returns the empty set, and the automaton stays halted \
for the rest of the string.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"86cc569a-be37-4aff-8a57-73ac76739a7f"],

